{
  "summary": "Deep research on four gaps in orin-dx/agent-plugins: few-shot examples, schema manifest declarations, long-context handling, and prompt injection resistance",
  "agentCount": 5,
  "logs": [],
  "result": {
    "topics": [
      {
        "topic": "Few-Shot Examples in proof-scanner and proof-adversary Agent Prompts",
        "key_findings": [
          {
            "finding": "The hazard reference files already function as runtime few-shot material for the scanner — adding duplicate examples to the system prompt is non-additive",
            "evidence": "Research (2024) shows 'the interaction between few-shot examples and contextual information is non-additive, with the gap between rich and poor prompts shrinking monotonically as the number of examples increases.' The rust-hazards.md and typescript-hazards.md files loaded at runtime via <load_first> already contain before/after code blocks for every taxonomy (T1-T10). These ARE the scanner's few-shot signal for pattern shape.",
            "relevance_to_repo": "proof-scanner already loads shared/references/rust-hazards.md or typescript-hazards.md at runtime. That file contains fenced code blocks showing the bad pattern and a corrected version for T7, T8, and T10. Adding the same examples to the proof-scanner prompt body wastes context budget and risks teaching the scanner to pre-filter based on pattern similarity — exactly the failure mode its backstory explicitly warns against ('scanners that tried to be clever')."
          },
          {
            "finding": "Few-shot examples improve LLM vulnerability detection measurably, but only when they demonstrate the agent's DECISION output, not just what the hazard looks like in code",
            "evidence": "The CMU SEI paper 'Using LLMs to Adjudicate Static-Analysis Alerts' found that LLMs show 'promising initial results in adjudicating static analysis alerts and providing rationales,' with hybrid LLM+static approaches eliminating 94-98% of false positives. The ZeroFalse framework achieves F1-scores of 0.912-0.955 by enriching alerts with 'flow-sensitive traces, contextual evidence, and CWE-specific knowledge before adjudication by an LLM.' The key: examples must be tailored to the adjudication decision, not just the code pattern.",
            "relevance_to_repo": "proof-adversary is the adjudication agent. The distinction between 'what T7 looks like in code' (covered by the hazard reference) and 'what a correctly confirmed vs. correctly dismissed T7 verdict looks like as JSON output with the decisive evidence sentence' is NOT currently covered anywhere. This gap is where few-shot examples add unique value."
          },
          {
            "finding": "Contrastive examples (confirmed + dismissed pair) outperform positive-only examples for binary classification judgment tasks",
            "evidence": "Research on Contrastive In-Context Learning (AAAI 2024, arxiv 2401.17390) shows that 'for each few-shot example, providing both a positive and a negative example significantly improves performance of LLMs in generating desirable responses.' The negative example defines the boundary. For an adversarial agent whose key failure mode is 'shallow reading — confirming based on the candidate's location without tracing actual paths,' a dismissed example showing the decisive refutation evidence is more calibrating than a confirmed example alone.",
            "relevance_to_repo": "proof-adversary's <judgment> section already names its failure mode as shallow reading. A contrastive pair — one confirmed finding with a populated trigger_condition, one dismissed finding with its refutation_evidence — anchors what 'concrete evidence' means in this repo's specific output schema. The dismissed example is more important than the confirmed one here because proof-adversary defaults to skepticism."
          },
          {
            "finding": "T7 and T10 have the highest false-positive risk and benefit most from clarifying examples; T3, T1, and T3 have high noise but are self-filtering by structural grep patterns",
            "evidence": "Research on 'On the Difficulty of Selecting Few-Shot Examples for Effective LLM-based Vulnerability Detection' (NDSS 2024, arxiv 2510.27675) shows that few-shot example impact is highest for categories with ambiguous boundaries — those requiring contextual reasoning rather than syntactic matching. T7/T8 (intent-capture-to-execution discard) and T10 (error suppression) are the categories in this repo that require multi-step control flow reasoning to adjudicate. T1 (type assertion bypass), T3 (any propagation), T4 (discarded parameters by leading underscore) resolve via local syntactic context that the grep pattern already captures.",
            "relevance_to_repo": "In rust-hazards.md, T7's false positive check is 'Is the field intentionally unused with a documented reason?' — a judgment call. T10's distinguisher from T4 ('T10 is intentional-but-lossy') is a subtle semantic distinction that the hazard reference explains but doesn't demonstrate as a verdict. typescript-hazards.md repeats this same pattern. These two are the categories where an adjudication example genuinely changes the quality of proof-adversary's verdict."
          },
          {
            "finding": "Token cost for 2 compact examples in proof-adversary is justified; adding examples to proof-scanner is not justified",
            "evidence": "Anthropic's context engineering guidance (2025) establishes that 'context rot' is real — as tokens increase, recall degrades. The adversary runs on opus/high, one candidate at a time. Its per-invocation context is: system prompt + hazard reference (~3-4KB) + one candidate + code snippets read by tool calls. Adding 2 targeted examples at ~400-600 tokens represents roughly a 10-15% context overhead — acceptable for a judgment agent on Opus. The scanner runs on sonnet/medium over all live files. Its context is consumed by grep output volume; adding examples would compete with the match data it needs.",
            "relevance_to_repo": "The cognitive mode table in shared/agent-best-practices.md section 3 assigns 'Adversarial: default-to-skepticism, requires concrete failing scenario' to proof-adversary and 'Enumeration: mechanical completeness, no judgment' to proof-scanner. Examples calibrate judgment; they do not help enumeration. Applying them only to proof-adversary is consistent with the repo's own cognitive mode framework."
          },
          {
            "finding": "XML-wrapped examples outperform plain text in Claude prompts for structured output tasks; placement at the end of <output> sections leverages the recency attention effect",
            "evidence": "Research on structured prompting confirms 'XML prompts organizing information into clearly defined tags help the model distinguish between instructions, input content, and output expectations — especially effective in models like Claude, which are trained to interpret tag-delimited content more reliably.' Separately, the 'lost in the middle' finding establishes that LLMs 'assign higher importance to items near the start and end and less to those in the middle' of long contexts.",
            "relevance_to_repo": "The proof-adversary prompt already uses XML section tags (<backstory>, <goal>, <judgment>, <output>) matching the repo's 4-part agent structure. Examples should be placed as the final content inside the <output> section, after all EARS contracts, wrapped in <example> sub-tags that mirror the repo's existing XML conventions. This matches Claude's training signal and leverages recency for the material most likely to influence the verdict JSON shape."
          }
        ],
        "specific_recommendations": [
          {
            "recommendation": "Do not add few-shot examples to proof-scanner. The hazard reference files loaded via <load_first> already serve as the scanner's pattern exemplars. Adding examples to the scanner prompt would teach it to pre-filter by pattern similarity, violating its core invariant ('emit everything, let the adversary decide'). The scanner's EARS rule 'THE SYSTEM SHALL NEVER filter out a match based on surrounding context' is the correct design — no examples needed.",
            "where_to_apply": "plugins/proof/agents/proof-scanner.md — take no action",
            "effort": "low",
            "expected_impact": "Preserves scanner exhaustiveness. Prevents context budget erosion on sonnet/medium where grep output volume is the primary context consumer."
          },
          {
            "recommendation": "Add two contrastive verdict examples to proof-adversary's <output> section. Place them after all EARS contracts, wrapped in <example> tags. Example 1: a confirmed T7 finding showing a populated trigger_condition that names the specific execution function whose parameter list drops the field. Example 2: a dismissed T7 candidate showing a refutation_evidence that names the exact line where the field IS read at the execution site. This pair anchors the confirmed/dismissed boundary without duplicating the code-pattern content already in the hazard reference.",
            "where_to_apply": "plugins/proof/agents/proof-adversary.md — append inside <output> section, after the EARS contracts",
            "effort": "medium",
            "expected_impact": "Reduces shallow-confirmation false positives for T7 (the highest-impact hazard in both Rust and TypeScript). Calibrates what 'concrete failing scenario' means as a populated JSON field versus a vague description. Estimated ~400-600 token overhead per adversary invocation — justified at opus/high tier."
          },
          {
            "recommendation": "Add a second contrastive pair for T10 specifically. T10 (error suppression/downgrade) has the most operationally subtle distinguisher from T4 (missing error propagation): 'T4 is accidental, T10 is intentional-but-lossy — different fix.' Example 1: a confirmed T10 dismissed as T4 but correctly identified as T10 because the catch closure receives the error and discards it (trigger: map_err(|_err|)). Example 2: a dismissed T10 where the catch is at an FFI boundary with a comment documenting why string conversion is required. These two cases are where proof-adversary's constitution sweep logic and the T10/T4 distinguisher most often lead to misclassification.",
            "where_to_apply": "plugins/proof/agents/proof-adversary.md — second <example> block inside <output>, immediately after the T7 pair",
            "effort": "medium",
            "expected_impact": "Reduces T4/T10 misclassification. The T10 distinguisher is currently prose-only in the hazard reference; a verdict example makes it behaviorally concrete for the adversary's output JSON."
          },
          {
            "recommendation": "Format examples as minimal JSON output objects with a one-sentence evidence annotation, not as full code reproductions. Do not copy the before/after code blocks from the hazard reference into the agent prompt — the adversary already loads that file. The example should show only: the decisive evidence sentence that drove the verdict, and the populated JSON fields (trigger_condition for confirmed; refutation_evidence for dismissed). Example format: <example label='T7 confirmed'>{ 'verdict': 'confirmed', 'trigger_condition': 'publish() accepts (id, version) only; plan.registry and plan.tag are dropped at line 42 of execute() and never appear in Command::new() arguments' }</example>. This keeps each example under 100 tokens.",
            "where_to_apply": "plugins/proof/agents/proof-adversary.md — example content format",
            "effort": "low",
            "expected_impact": "Keeps total example overhead under 400 tokens for two pairs. Avoids context rot from duplicating large code blocks already present in the hazard reference. The JSON-first format directly models the output shape the adversary must produce, reinforcing schema compliance."
          },
          {
            "recommendation": "Update the hazard reference files (rust-hazards.md, typescript-hazards.md) to add a 'Verdict signal' sub-field to T7 and T10 entries — a one-sentence statement of what evidence makes the finding confirmable vs. dismissible at the execution boundary. This is distinct from the existing 'False positive check' field. The reference file is runtime-loaded by the adversary; augmenting it there is more token-efficient than embedding the same content in the agent prompt, and it keeps the single source of truth in one place.",
            "where_to_apply": "shared/references/rust-hazards.md and shared/references/typescript-hazards.md — T7 and T10 sections",
            "effort": "low",
            "expected_impact": "Improves adversary judgment at zero agent-prompt token cost. The 'Verdict signal' field directly answers the adjudication question ('what evidence is sufficient to confirm vs. dismiss?') in the same file the adversary already loads. This is the lowest-cost, most repo-consistent improvement available."
          }
        ],
        "sources": [
          "https://arxiv.org/html/2510.27675 — On the Difficulty of Selecting Few-Shot Examples for Effective LLM-based Vulnerability Detection (NDSS 2024)",
          "https://arxiv.org/html/2510.02534 — ZeroFalse: Improving Precision in Static Analysis with LLMs",
          "https://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/wklieber/LLMs-to-Adjudicate-Static-Analysis-Alerts.pdf — CMU SEI: Using LLMs to Adjudicate Static-Analysis Alerts",
          "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.17390 — Customizing Language Model Responses with Contrastive In-Context Learning (AAAI 2024)",
          "https://arxiv.org/html/2601.22952v1 — Sifting the Noise: A Comparative Study of LLM Agents in Vulnerability False Positive Filtering",
          "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents — Anthropic: Effective Context Engineering for AI Agents",
          "https://claude.com/blog/best-practices-for-prompt-engineering — Anthropic: Prompt Engineering Best Practices 2026",
          "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.00245 — The Hitchhiker's Guide to Program Analysis: A Journey with Large Language Models",
          "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.10104 — Expert-in-the-Loop Systems with Cross-Domain and In-Domain Few-Shot Learning for Software Vulnerability Detection",
          "https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/29760/31308 — Contrastive In-Context Learning (AAAI proceedings)",
          "https://thakur.cs.ucdavis.edu/assets/pubs/STTT2025.pdf — Interleaving Static Analysis and LLM Prompting"
        ],
        "reasoning": "The research question has two distinct subjects (proof-scanner and proof-adversary) that require opposite answers, which is the most important architectural insight.\n\nFor proof-scanner: The hazard reference files are already structured as runtime few-shot material. rust-hazards.md and typescript-hazards.md contain explicit before/after code examples for every taxonomy. The scanner's cognitive mode is mechanical enumeration — its backstory and EARS constraints explicitly prohibit contextual filtering. Adding examples to the scanner prompt would work against its design by providing a pattern-matching shortcut that substitutes for exhaustive grep execution. Research confirms that few-shot examples are non-additive when the context already contains equivalent exemplars (2024 finding on interaction between few-shot and contextual information). The correct answer for the scanner is: no examples in the prompt, no changes needed.\n\nFor proof-adversary: This is where examples provide genuine, non-redundant value. The hazard reference tells the adversary what the code pattern looks like and what the false positive checks are. It does NOT show what a correctly populated confirmed finding-report@1 JSON looks like alongside the decisive evidence sentence, nor does it show what a correctly dismissed candidate looks like with its specific refutation evidence. This gap — between 'knowing the hazard' and 'knowing how to express a verdict' — is exactly what few-shot examples address. Contrastive learning research (AAAI 2024) shows that negative examples (correctly dismissed cases) are particularly important for boundary-setting in binary classification, which aligns with the adversary's default-to-skepticism posture.\n\nThe hazard categories that benefit most from examples are T7 and T10, because both require multi-step control flow reasoning with subtle false-positive boundaries. T7's false positive ('Is the field intentionally unused with a documented reason?') requires the adversary to distinguish structural type compatibility from behavioral field usage — a judgment distinction that prose alone may not convey precisely enough. T10's distinguisher from T4 ('intentional-but-lossy vs. accidental') is similarly fine-grained. By contrast, T1 (type assertions), T3 (any propagation), and T4 (discarded parameters by leading underscore) resolve by local syntactic evidence that the grep pattern captures directly, making them lower priority for adjudication examples.\n\nThe format recommendation (minimal JSON output objects with a one-sentence evidence annotation) is driven by three constraints: (1) the adversary already loads the full hazard reference with code examples, so duplicating code content wastes tokens and invites context rot, (2) the format must not violate the repo's writing quality rule against 'procedure masquerading as guidance,' and (3) the output-JSON-first format directly reinforces schema compliance for finding-report@1 and the dismissal object, which is the adversary's actual deliverable.\n\nThe 'Verdict signal' field recommendation for the hazard reference files is the single highest-value, lowest-cost intervention in this repo. It augments the runtime-loaded context (which both scanner and adversary consume) rather than the agent prompt, keeping the single source of truth intact and avoiding any per-prompt token overhead beyond what the reference file already costs."
      },
      {
        "topic": "Schema/capability declaration in AI agent plugin manifests and wiring-time validation — recommendations for orin-dx/agent-plugins",
        "key_findings": [
          {
            "finding": "VS Code contributes.jsonValidation: manifest declares file pattern → schema URL bindings, not data-flow contracts",
            "evidence": "The jsonValidation contribution point takes {fileMatch, url} pairs. url can be local (./schemas/foo.schema.json) or remote (schemastore.org). This tells the editor which schema validates which file type. There is no produces/consumes pattern — extensions do not declare what data they hand off to other extensions. Stability is communicated through documentation labels ('proposed API'), not manifest fields. The pattern is: capability declaration is in the manifest, but data-flow contracts are not.",
            "relevance_to_repo": "The repo's plugin.json already mirrors the VS Code pattern — it declares what the plugin IS (id, version, agents list) but not what it hands to or receives from adjacent plugins. VS Code's solution to data-flow is type-checking in code (language servers), not manifest fields. This repo needs a different approach because agents are prose-driven, not code-driven."
          },
          {
