        "reasoning": "The research question asks whether plugin.json should declare produces/consumes schema references, what format to use, and how other ecosystems handle wiring-time compatibility. Here is the full reasoning chain.\n\nTHE CORE GAP\n\nEvery plugin in this repo has a clearly defined schema boundary — what typed JSON it accepts and what it emits — but that boundary exists only in SKILL.md prose and agent <output> sections. The produces/consumes map is already documented (vector SKILL.md explicitly writes 'Consumes: spec@1, Produces: plan@1'); it is just not machine-readable. The gap is a single promotion: structured data into plugin.json.\n\nECOSYSTEM SURVEY CONCLUSIONS\n\nVS Code extensions use contributes.jsonValidation to bind file patterns to schema URLs, but this is an editor hint, not a data-flow contract. Extensions do not declare what data they hand off to other extensions. The VS Code contribution point system is relevant only as a precedent for declarative capability registration in a manifest, not as a produces/consumes model.\n\nHaystack 2.x is the closest functional analog. Its @component.output_types() decorator and typed run() parameters are exactly what this repo needs, projected into JSON. Pipeline.connect() validates type compatibility at wiring time before any model is invoked. The validation is structural (type names must match), not semantic. This is achievable with a simple jq script against plugin.json.\n\nLangGraph is not directly applicable because its shared-state model assumes all nodes are in the same graph with shared memory. This repo's plugins are independently installable units, which requires edge-level contracts rather than a shared state type.\n\nAgent Plugins 1.0.0 (the new open standard launched August 6, 2026, the same day this research was requested) explicitly does NOT define produces/consumes in v1.0.0. The spec lists capability declarations as a known gap. Adding these fields to plugin.json is a forward-looking first-party extension that does not conflict with the standard.\n\nKafka Schema Registry provides the right governance model: a schema cannot change in a breaking way without a version bump, enforced at registration time. For this repo: a pre-commit hook or CI step that runs validate-wiring.sh serves the same enforcement role.\n\nMCP tool definitions include an inputSchema but no outputSchema. The broader consensus across ecosystems (MCP, ADK, OpenAI plugins) is: manifests identify plugins, schema files are the contract. This repo already follows this pattern — the gap is that plugin.json does not reference the schema files.\n\nVERSION EXPRESSION FORMAT\n\nThe @N integer convention already in use is the right choice. Do not adopt semver ranges (>=1.0.0). Reasons: (1) all schemas have additionalProperties: false, meaning any structural change is breaking by definition — a semver minor bump would be misleading; (2) the @N notation is already the $id format in every schema file and the naming convention for schema files; (3) integer major versions are the correct signal for 'this is a breaking change, update both ends'. Semver ranges are appropriate for libraries where backward compatibility is maintained; they are not appropriate for strict JSON contracts.\n\nPLUGIN-LEVEL VS AGENT-LEVEL DECLARATIONS\n\nBoth levels serve distinct purposes and are not mutually exclusive. Plugin-level produces/consumes declares the external surface — what a pipeline orchestrator needs to know to wire plugins together. Agent-level declarations (in YAML frontmatter) document the internal wiring — which agent within a plugin handles which schema. The plugin is the unit of installation; the agent is the unit of dispatch. Both need their contracts declared.\n\nVALIDATION TIMING\n\nInstall time: check that all schema $id strings in consumes/produces resolve to actual files in shared/schemas/. This is a filesystem check, no network required, runs in under a second with jq and find.\n\nWiring time: when a user declares a pipeline (graph → trace → canon → vector → lambda → axiom → delta), check pairwise that producer.produces satisfies consumer.consumes for all non-optional entries. This is the Haystack Pipeline.connect() equivalent.\n\nRuntime: JSON Schema validation already happens via additionalProperties: false in the schema files themselves. Agents that output spec@1 JSON are validated against shared/schemas/spec@1.json. No new runtime infrastructure is needed.\n\nSTABILITY LIFECYCLE\n\nThe proposed/ directory already signals experimental, but the signal is only in the directory path. Adding 'stability': 'experimental' to the schema JSON itself makes the signal self-describing — a tool reading the schema file does not need to know the directory convention. Plugin.json references to experimental schemas should generate warnings, not errors, during wiring validation. This mirrors how Anthropic marks proposed Claude Code APIs in documentation — the signal belongs in the artifact.\n\nTHE CONCRETE PRODUCES/CONSUMES MAP\n\nFrom reading SKILL.md files and agent <output> sections:\ngraph: consumes nothing (free text input), produces requirement@1\ntrace: consumes requirement@1, produces research-report@1\ncanon: consumes requirement@1 (required), research-report@1 (optional), finding-report@1 (optional via canon/architect), produces spec@1 and verdict@1\nvector: consumes spec@1, produces plan@1\nlambda: consumes plan@1 (preferred) or spec@1 (optional fallback), produces changeset@1 and verdict@1\naxiom: consumes any artifact type (cross-cutting gate), produces verdict@1\ndelta: consumes changeset@1 and git diff (not a schema), produces release-artifact@1\nproof: consumes codebase (no schema input), produces finding-report@1 and verdict@1\n\nThis map is the direct input to the plugin.json additions. The optional flags and the via annotations (e.g., finding-report@1 is only consumed by canon/architect, not by canon/drafter) are important for accurate wiring validation — a validator should not reject a canon installation that has no finding-report@1 upstream, because that schema is optional."
